  "notes": "A relatively formal way to express anger. Composed of {立|りつ} (to rise) + {腹|ぷく} (belly/stomach), literally \"the stomach rises\" — related to the Japanese association of the belly with emotions.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {立腹|りっぷく}する: to be angered, to take offense\n- {大変|たいへん}{立腹|りっぷく}する: to be very angry\n- ご{立腹|りっぷく}: your anger (respectful)\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {怒|いか}り: anger — the most general word for anger\n- {憤慨|ふんがい}: indignation — anger at something perceived as unjust\n- {激怒|げきど}: fury, rage — much stronger intensity\n\nREGISTER:\n{立腹|りっぷく} is more formal and restrained than {怒|おこ}る. It often appears in business or formal contexts, especially in the respectful form ご{立腹|りっぷく}.",
